The phone viewport is the boundary for all end-user screens and overlays. The controls below the phone are research/demo controls, not part of the consumer product experience.

---

## Screen Wireframes

### 1. Photos Feed

```text
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ Account              More    │
│                              │
│ [Memory] [Memory] [Memory] → │  Memories carousel
│                              │
│             Today            │
│ [photo] [photo] [photo]      │
│ [photo] [photo] [photo]      │
│                              │
│           Yesterday          │
│ [photo] [photo] [photo]      │  Scrollable, date-grouped grid
│                              │
│                         [clue]│  Optional transient clues
│                         [clue]│
│ [Photos] [Collections] [Create] [Search]
└──────────────────────────────┘
```

- The photo grid is ordered newest first and grouped by relative date headers.
- Selecting a feed photo or a Memory opens the photo viewer for that item.
- The active Photos item, Collections item, or Create item is shown in the floating navigation pill.
- Tapping the round Search button opens Search and consumes any visible floating clue stack.
- Scrolling the Photos feed dismisses visible floating clues.

### 2. Photo Viewer

```text
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ [Close]                [Info]│
│                              │
│                              │
│          PHOTO               │  Horizontal swipe changes photos
│                              │
│                              │
│       [ Add a note ]         │  Appears after about 1 second when eligible
│                              │
│ [Share] [Edit] [Lens] [More] │
└──────────────────────────────┘
```

- A small Add a note prompt is scheduled for an unnoted photo that has not already been prompted. It appears after about 1 second and times out after about 5 seconds if ignored.
- Tapping the prompt expands it in place. The editor supports up to 60 characters, a character counter, contextual suggestion chips, and Save. Selecting a suggestion marks the note source as a suggestion; otherwise the source is typed.
- Saving stores the note against the photo, collapses the prompt, and displays a brief “Note saved” confirmation.
- Close returns to the originating screen. Horizontal swipes move through the current viewer list.
- Once a photo has been prompted, the automatic prompt does not appear again for that photo. A saved note also suppresses it. The Photo notes setting can disable prompts.

### 3. Photo Information Sheet and Note Editor

```text
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ Viewer remains behind sheet  │
│                              │
│       ┌────────────────┐     │
│       │       ─        │     │
│       │ Info           │     │
│       │ Date and time  │     │
│       │ Place · city   │     │
│       │ People         │     │
│       │                │     │
│       │ Note text      │     │
│       │ [Edit] [Delete]│     │
│       └────────────────┘     │
└──────────────────────────────┘
```

When no note exists, the note area shows “No note” and **Add note**. Selecting Add note or Edit opens the same 60-character input with Save and Cancel. Saving an empty value removes the note. Selecting Delete removes an existing note. Clicking outside the sheet dismisses it.

### 4. Collections and Notes

```text
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ Account       Collections More│
│ [Favorites] [Trash]          │
│                              │
│ [People & pets] [Places]     │
│ [Documents]   [Albums]       │
│ [Moments]     [Screenshots]  │
│ [Notes]                     │
│                              │
│ [Photos] [Collections] [Create] [Search]
└──────────────────────────────┘
```

Selecting Notes opens notes grouped by month:

```text
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ [Back] Notes                 │
│ October 2026                 │
│ [thumb] Note phrase          │
│         Oct 4 · Place        │
│         [Edit]               │
│                              │
│ September 2026               │
│ [thumb] Another note         │
│         Sep 12 · Place       │
│         [Edit]               │
└──────────────────────────────┘
```

- Opening a note row opens its photo in the viewer.
- Edit opens an inline field limited to 60 characters. Save persists the change; saving a blank value removes the note. Cancel abandons the draft.
- With no saved notes, the page presents an empty state explaining that added notes will appear here.
- Other collection destinations include People & pets, Places, Albums, Moments, Documents, Screenshots, Favorites, and Trash. Where relevant, selecting an item opens a filtered photo grid and selecting a photo opens the viewer.

### 5. Search / Ask

```text
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ [Back] [query token] [input] │
│                              │
│ [ Search ] [ Ask ]           │
│                              │
│ Suggested clues              │  Visible before text is entered
│ [From your notes] [Where]    │
│ [Who] [When] [Kind]          │
│                              │
│ Matching photo grid          │
│ [photo] [photo] [photo]      │
└──────────────────────────────┘
```

- Search begins with a focused input. As the user types, matching autocomplete suggestions can be selected as tokens; tokens can be removed.
- Search and Ask are separate modes. Ask offers clue starters when empty; Search shows clue chips when empty.
- Search uses photo notes and library metadata such as place, city, people, date, photo kind, album, and tags. Local semantic/concept scoring provides the fallback. A configured server model may additionally rank metadata matches.
- Results are ranked photo tiles. A photo whose note supports the query can show a **Your note** label with the note text.
- Selecting a result opens the photo viewer with the result list as its swipe sequence. Back closes Search.

### 6. Floating Clue Results

```text
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ [Back] [selected clue token] │
│                              │
│ [photo] [photo] [photo]      │
│ [Your note: phrase]          │
│ [photo] [photo] [photo]      │
└──────────────────────────────┘
```

- Returning from the simulated capture screen to Photos schedules a temporary stack of up to three clue chips above the round Search button.
- A clue tap opens this compact results screen with the selected clue represented as a search token.
- Back returns to the library. Selecting a photo logs a result tap and elapsed time-to-find, then opens that photo in the viewer.
- Clues time out, disappear on feed scroll, disappear when Search opens, or are consumed when one is selected. The external Clue chips toggle controls this prototype behavior.

### 7. Simulated Capture

```text
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ [Back]                       │
│                              │
│          FULL PHOTO          │
│                              │
│        [Add a note]          │  Appears as soon as capture completes
│                              │
│       [Share] [Edit] [Delete]│
└──────────────────────────────┘
```

- The outside **Simulate new photo** control opens a full-screen captured-photo state using the prototype’s first library item.
- The Add a note pill appears immediately after the new photo is captured. If untouched, it fades away after 2 seconds. It is 32px tall with 12px text.
- Tapping expands the pill to an input with a 60-character counter, two photo place/time suggestions, and Save. There is no microphone control.
- Saving collapses the pill and briefly displays “Note saved.” Back or a horizontal swipe returns to Photos; the pill is dismissed immediately and floating clues are scheduled after return.
- The Share and Edit actions are visual affordances only in the current capture prototype. Delete returns to the library; it does not modify the sample library.

### 8. Settings

```text
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ [Back] Settings              │
│                              │
│ Photo notes                  │
│ Suggest a short note on new  │
│ photos                [toggle]│
└──────────────────────────────┘
```

Settings are reachable from the account avatar or More menu. The Photo notes toggle enables or disables automatic note prompts. The description is prototype copy; the actual prompt gate applies to eligible unnoted feed photos.

---

## End-to-End User Journeys

### Journey A: Add a Note While Browsing

**User intent:** attach a remembered phrase to a photo before moving on.

1. User opens Photos and scrolls the date-grouped library or selects a Memory.
2. User opens a photo. The viewer schedules an Add a note prompt if Photo notes is enabled and the photo is not already prompted or noted.
3. After about 1 second, the prompt appears. If ignored, it times out after about 5 seconds and the user stays in the viewer.
4. User taps Add a note. The compact pill expands to the editor and the input receives focus.
5. User types a phrase (up to 60 characters) or selects a contextual suggestion.
6. User selects Save. The note is persisted, a success confirmation appears briefly, and the prompt is no longer shown for that photo.
7. User closes the viewer or continues browsing. The saved note is available in the Info sheet, Notes collection, and search matching.

**Alternate paths:** user closes or swipes while the prompt is visible (prompt is dismissed and ignored); user cancels from Info editing (no change); blank Info save deletes the note.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Photos feed] --> B[Open photo]
    B --> C{Prompt eligible?}
    C -->|No| D[Browse photo / open Info]
    C -->|Yes| E[Wait about 1 second]
    E --> F[Add a note pill]
    F -->|Ignore for about 5 seconds| G[Dismiss prompt]
    F -->|Tap| H[Expand editor]
    H --> I{Type or choose suggestion}
    I --> J[Save note]
    J --> K[Persist note and show confirmation]
    K --> L[Find later in Notes or Search]
```

### Journey B: Add or Edit a Note from Photo Info

**User intent:** add or correct a note while inspecting photo details.

1. Open a photo and tap Info (or More in the bottom viewer actions).
2. Review date/time, location, and people.
3. If the photo has no note, select Add note. If it has a note, select Edit.
4. Edit up to 60 characters; select Save or Cancel.
5. Save returns to the Info view with the persisted note. Delete removes an existing note.

### Journey C: Edit an Older Note from Collections

**User intent:** locate and update a note without finding the photo in the feed.

1. Select Collections from the bottom navigation.
2. Open Notes.
3. Scan notes grouped by month, using each thumbnail, date, and place as context.
4. Select Edit on the desired row.
5. Update the text and Save, or choose Cancel.
6. The list shows the updated note; the photo remains accessible by opening its row.

### Journey D: Rediscover a Photo with Search

**User intent:** find a photo using partial memory rather than an exact file/date.

1. Tap Search from Photos.
2. Enter a natural-language clue (for example, `cafe meetup`) or select a suggested clue.
3. Review autocomplete and select a token if useful; continue typing to refine.
4. Review ranked photo results and any **Your note** labels.
5. Open a result. The viewer opens on that match; the search journey logs result selection and time-to-find.
6. Close the viewer or search to return to the photo library.

### Journey E: Rediscover via a Floating Clue

**User intent:** reuse a recent note/date/place clue immediately after capture.

1. Start from Photos and select the outside demo control **Simulate new photo**.
2. The Add a note pill appears immediately. Tap it before it fades after 2 seconds, then optionally save a note.
3. Return with Back or swipe.
4. After about 1 second, review the floating clue stack above Search.
5. Tap a clue to open clue-token results; select a photo to view it.
6. Return to Photos. The current clue set is consumed and does not reappear until a new capture/session trigger.

### Journey F: Browse a Collection

**User intent:** browse a semantic grouping instead of searching text.

1. Select Collections.
2. Choose People & pets, Places, Albums, Moments, Documents, Screenshots, Favorites, Trash, or Notes.
3. Select a person/place/album/moment/category to open a filtered grid where applicable.
4. Select a photo to open the viewer; use swipe to move within the current result list.
5. Use Back to return to the collection list or its parent grouping.

---

## States and Decision Rules

| Area | State or trigger | User-visible result |
|---|---|---|
| Feed | Initial load | Photos grouped by date, Memories row, bottom navigation |
| Viewer prompt | Photo notes off | No automatic note prompt |
| Viewer prompt | Photo already prompted or already has a note | No automatic prompt |
| Viewer prompt | Unnoted and not previously prompted | Prompt appears after about 1 second |
| Prompt | Ignored | Prompt fades/dismisses after about 5 seconds |
| Capture note pill | New photo captured | Pill appears immediately and fades after 2 seconds if untouched |
| Prompt | Expanded | Note field, counter, contextual suggestions, Save |
| Notes collection | No saved notes | Empty state |
| Notes collection | One or more saved notes | Notes grouped by month, each row openable/editable |
| Search | Empty input | Search clue chips or Ask starter clues |
| Search | Input text | Autocomplete suggestions and ranked matches |
| Results | Note supports match | Tile can include **Your note** and note text |
| Floating clues | Returned from simulated capture | Up to three chips after about 1 second |
| Floating clues | Scroll, search, tap, or timeout | Chips are removed/consumed |
| Capture | Note saved | Pill collapses; “Note saved” confirmation appears briefly |
| Capture | Back or horizontal swipe | Return to Photos; schedule floating clues |

---

## Data and Persistence

- Sample photos carry an identifier, image source, capture timestamp, place/city, people, kind, album, and tags.
- Notes are associated with photo IDs and persisted in browser `localStorage` under `gp-notes-proto-v1`.
- Prompt history, feature toggles, ignored-prompt state, and the event log are persisted in the same browser storage.
- Search combines note text with available metadata. The current sample library and local scoring are demonstration-scale, not a production index.

---

## Instrumentation

The prototype event log records user actions and search behavior. Note/capture-related events include `pill_shown`, `pill_expanded`, `pill_ignored`, and `note_saved` (including source and seconds to save). Rediscovery events include `floating_chip_shown`, `floating_chip_tap`, `result_tap`, and `time_to_find`. Other search events may include query entry/reformulation and search open/abandonment.

**Suggested MVP outcome measures:**

- Note prompt expansion rate: expanded prompts ÷ shown prompts.
- Note completion rate: saved notes ÷ expanded prompts.
- Prompt dismissal rate and time-to-save by note source.
- Clue engagement rate: clue taps ÷ clue impressions.
- Retrieval success rate: photo result opens ÷ search/clue journeys.
- Median time-to-find, segmented by typed search versus floating clue.
- Note edit/delete rate as a signal of note quality or correction need.

---

## Prototype Boundaries and Open Decisions

- Reset, Seed example note, Simulate new photo, Clue chips on/off, and Event log are lab controls outside the phone frame; hide or replace them in a production app.
- The seeded note is demo data. Reset clears locally persisted state and event history.
- Share/Edit in the simulated capture action row are visual-only in the current prototype. Delete exits capture but does not delete library data.
- The capture path uses the first sample-library photo as its simulated newly captured photo. This is a deterministic demo fixture, not a real camera integration.
- Search is a hybrid demo: local concept/keyword scoring is available; server-side semantic ranking depends on backend/API configuration.
- Notes and prompt history are browser-local. No account sync, cross-device persistence, photo upload, permissions, or production privacy controls are represented.
- The ordinary viewer note prompt and the simulated capture note pill are separate implementations: the viewer prompt appears after about 1 second and fades after about 5 seconds; the capture pill appears immediately and fades after 2 seconds. Keep this distinction explicit or unify their timing before production.
- Confirm product policy for repeat prompts, dismissal frequency limits, prompt behavior after editing/deleting a note, and whether floating clues should include photo place versus city.
